Authenticity fixtures for td-boot's ed25519 verifier.

td-boot has the verifier and deliberately not the signer, so it cannot produce
a signature in its own tests. The positive case is therefore this committed
triple, generated once by `td-deploy`:

    deployment.manifest      a canonical td-deployment-v1 manifest
    deployment.manifest.sig  its detached signature, hex + newline
    deployment.pub           the public half of the key that signed it
    deployment-other.pub     an unrelated public half, for the wrong-key case

NO PRIVATE KEY IS COMMITTED, and the private halves that made these no longer
exist. That is the point rather than an oversight: `td-install/DESIGN.md` §6
records why build-pinning a key and "no committed private key" cannot both
hold, and `tests/td-subst.pub` already establishes the practice of committing
a public half alone.

The consequence is that these files cannot be regenerated to sign a DIFFERENT
manifest — a new manifest needs a new keypair and a new signature, from
`td-deploy keygen` then `td-deploy sign`, with the private half discarded
afterwards. Nothing here has to match the payload bytes any test writes: the
manifest is verified as bytes, and the digests it names are never resolved.

Every NEGATIVE case (wrong key, tampered manifest, malformed or truncated
signature, absent key) needs no signer, which is why those are exhaustive and
this is not.

The BOOT path needs a second set, and cannot use these. The sentence above —
that nothing here has to match the payload bytes a test writes — is exactly
what makes these unusable for it: a deployment that boots must carry payloads
that hash to what its manifest names, so its manifest is decided by those
payload bytes rather than chosen. That set therefore lives beside the payloads
that decide it, as PUBLIC_KEY and SIGNATURES in td-boot/src/fixture.rs, signed
the same way and under the same rule: one key, every fixture manifest signed
with it, the private half discarded. The regeneration procedure is recorded
there because it is the payload constants next to it that force a
regeneration.

That file is its own module rather than part of td-boot's test module because
the td-install recipe check stages the SAME deployment and needs the same
signatures: it is #[cfg(test)] in td-boot and #[path]-included as ordinary code
by recipes/src/recipes/td-install-test.rs. One definition, because a second
copy of the payload bytes would drift under a signature that still verified for
the other — and the direction that fails is quiet, a staged deployment td-boot
refuses and a check that reports a broken installer.
